+<p>Form 941 does <strong>not</strong> report federal unemployment tax — that is <a href="/blog/how-to-file-form-940.html">Form 940</a>, filed annually. It does not report backup withholding or withholding on nonpayroll payments such as pensions — that is Form 945. And it does not cover agricultural labor (Form 943) or household employees (usually Schedule H). If you are not sure which form applies, the Instructions for Form 941, not a vendor blog, are the authority.</p>
 <p>One point employers get wrong: <strong>filing and depositing are separate steps.</strong> Form 941 is the quarterly report. The tax money itself is deposited during the quarter, on a monthly or semiweekly schedule, through EFTPS or another electronic method. By the time you file, the balance due on the return should usually be zero — the deposits have already paid it. If it is not zero, something went wrong during the quarter, and the return is where it shows up.</p>
 
